{"subjectId":"c","id":"structs","title":"Structures","group":"Structures & User-Defined Types","order":34,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <code>struct</code> groups related values of different types into one object — C’s building block for records, and for every data structure.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Define, initialise, access</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nstruct Student {\n    int id;\n    char name[20];\n    double gpa;\n};\n\nint main(void) {\n    struct Student a = {1, \"Asha\", 8.9};                        /* positional */\n    struct Student b = {.name = \"Ravi\", .gpa = 7.5, .id = 2};   /* designated (C99) */\n    struct Student c = {0};                                     /* all zero */\n\n    c.id = 3;\n    strcpy(c.name, \"Meera\");\n    c.gpa = 9.2;\n\n    struct Student *p = &amp;c;\n    p-&gt;gpa += 0.1;                     /* p-&gt;gpa is (*p).gpa */\n\n    printf(\"%d %s %.1f\\n\", a.id, a.name, a.gpa);\n    printf(\"%d %s %.1f\\n\", b.id, b.name, b.gpa);\n    printf(\"%d %s %.1f\\n\", p-&gt;id, (*p).name, p-&gt;gpa);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 Asha 8.9\n2 Ravi 7.5\n3 Meera 9.3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Use with</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>.</code></td><td>a struct object</td><td><code>s.gpa</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>-&gt;</code></td><td>a pointer to a struct</td><td><code>p-&gt;gpa</code> ≡ <code>(*p).gpa</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Copying, passing and returning</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct { double x, y; } Point;\n\nPoint midpoint(Point a, Point b) {          /* by value: copies in, copy out */\n    return (Point){(a.x + b.x) / 2, (a.y + b.y) / 2};    /* compound literal */\n}\n\nvoid translate(Point *p, double dx, double dy) {         /* by pointer: modifies caller */\n    p-&gt;x += dx;\n    p-&gt;y += dy;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    Point a = {0, 0}, b = {4, 2};\n    Point m = midpoint(a, b);\n    Point copy = m;                 /* struct assignment copies every member */\n    translate(&amp;m, 1, 1);\n    printf(\"m=(%.1f,%.1f) copy=(%.1f,%.1f)\\n\", m.x, m.y, copy.x, copy.y);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>m=(3.0,2.0) copy=(2.0,1.0)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Structs <em>can</em> be assigned, passed and returned by value (arrays can’t). They <strong>cannot</strong> be compared with <code>==</code> — compare member by member. Pass large structs as <code>const T *</code> to avoid copying.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>Nested structs and arrays of structs</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nstruct Date { int d, m, y; };\nstruct Employee {\n    char name[16];\n    struct Date joined;           /* nested */\n    double salary;\n};\n\nint main(void) {\n    struct Employee staff[] = {\n        {\"Anil\", {1, 4, 2020}, 50000},\n        {\"Bina\", {15, 9, 2018}, 72000},\n        {\"Chen\", {3, 1, 2022}, 61000},\n    };\n    int n = sizeof staff / sizeof staff[0];\n    double total = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        total += staff[i].salary;\n        printf(\"%-5s joined %02d/%02d/%d\\n\", staff[i].name,\n               staff[i].joined.d, staff[i].joined.m, staff[i].joined.y);\n    }\n    printf(\"average salary %.0f\\n\", total / n);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Anil  joined 01/04/2020\nBina  joined 15/09/2018\nChen  joined 03/01/2022\naverage salary 61000</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Self-referential structs</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int value;\n    struct Node *next;        /* a pointer to its own type is fine; a Node member is not */\n};\n\nint main(void) {\n    struct Node c = {3, NULL}, b = {2, &amp;c}, a = {1, &amp;b};\n    for (struct Node *n = &amp;a; n != NULL; n = n-&gt;next) printf(\"%d -&gt; \", n-&gt;value);\n    printf(\"NULL\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; NULL</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-horizontal\"></i>Flexible array member (C99)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    size_t len;\n    char data[];               /* flexible array member: must be last */\n} Buffer;\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *text = \"variable length\";\n    size_t n = strlen(text) + 1;\n    Buffer *b = malloc(sizeof *b + n);   /* header + payload in ONE allocation */\n    if (!b) return 1;\n    b-&gt;len = n - 1;\n    memcpy(b-&gt;data, text, n);\n    printf(\"%zu: %s (sizeof Buffer = %zu)\\n\", b-&gt;len, b-&gt;data, sizeof(Buffer));\n    free(b);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>15: variable length (sizeof Buffer = 8)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Opaque types: encapsulation in C</h3>\n<p>Declaring <code>struct Stack;</code> in a header without its body (an <em>incomplete type</em>) lets users hold <code>struct Stack *</code> but not touch the fields — only functions in <code>stack.c</code> can. This is how <code>FILE</code> works.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — header of an opaque type (compile-checked)</figcaption><pre><code>/* stack.h */\ntypedef struct Stack Stack;           /* opaque: fields hidden */\nStack *stack_new(void);\nvoid   stack_push(Stack *s, int v);\nint    stack_pop(Stack *s);\nvoid   stack_free(Stack *s);</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between . and -&gt; ?</summary><div><p><code>.</code> accesses a member of a struct <em>object</em>; <code>-&gt;</code> accesses a member through a <em>pointer</em> to a struct. <code>p-&gt;m</code> is shorthand for <code>(*p).m</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can you compare two structs with ==?</summary><div><p>No — C does not define <code>==</code> for structs. Compare members individually. <code>memcmp</code> is unreliable because padding bytes have unspecified values (and floating-point <code>-0.0</code>/<code>NaN</code> compare differently from their bytes).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why can't a struct contain a member of its own type?</summary><div><p>It would have infinite size. It can contain a <em>pointer</em> to its own type, because a pointer has a fixed size — that is how linked lists and trees are built.</p></div></details></section>"}